From 4a1b1ac2744694a2ecd66a84bdb1445f4ef24bee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jason Gunthorpe <jgg@nvidia.com>
Date: Tue, 26 May 2026 12:40:25 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] RDMA/core: Validate the passed in fops for ib_get_ucaps()

Sashiko pointed out it is not safe to rely only on the devt because
char/block alias so if the user finds a block device with the same dev_t
it can masquerade as a ucap cdev fd.

Test the f_ops to only accept authentic cdevs.

Link: https://patch.msgid.link/r/0-v1-fd9482545e37+1e25-ib_ucaps_fd_ops_jgg@nvidia.com
Cc: stable@vger.kernel.org
Fixes: 61e51682816d ("RDMA/uverbs: Introduce UCAP (User CAPabilities) API")
Signed-off-by: Jason Gunthorpe <jgg@nvidia.com>

diff --git a/drivers/infiniband/core/ucaps.c b/drivers/infiniband/core/ucaps.c
index 948093260dbd..5155ff0e538e 100644
--- a/drivers/infiniband/core/ucaps.c
+++ b/drivers/infiniband/core/ucaps.c
@@ -82,14 +82,12 @@ static int get_ucap_from_devt(dev_t devt, u64 *idx_mask)

 static int get_devt_from_fd(unsigned int fd, dev_t *ret_dev)
 {
-	struct file *file;
+	CLASS(fd, f)(fd);

-	file = fget(fd);
-	if (!file)
+	if (fd_empty(f) || fd_file(f)->f_op != &ucaps_cdev_fops)
 		return -EBADF;

-	*ret_dev = file_inode(file)->i_rdev;
-	fput(file);
+	*ret_dev = file_inode(fd_file(f))->i_rdev;
 	return 0;
 }

-- 
2.34.1

The code statements that lead to the bug are:
<text>
file = fget(fd);
*ret_dev = file_inode(file)->i_rdev;
</text>
Reason:
The original code retrieves the dev_t from the file's inode without verifying that the file's f_ops match the expected ucap cdev operations (`&ucaps_cdev_fops`). A block device with the same dev_t can masquerade as a ucap cdev, leading to security issues.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
<text>
if (fd_empty(f) || fd_file(f)->f_op != &ucaps_cdev_fops)
    return -EBADF;
*ret_dev = file_inode(fd_file(f))->i_rdev;
</text>
Reason:
The fix adds a check comparing the file's f_op to the expected `ucaps_cdev_fops`. If the file does not have the correct operations (i.e., it is not an authentic ucap cdev), the function returns -EBADF, preventing block devices from impersonating ucap cdevs. The use of `CLASS(fd, f)(fd)` safely manages the file reference.

